## Secure Implementation

### Tier 1: Critical Fix (5 minutes)

Remove the dynamic configuration loading endpoint entirely and use Spring profiles for environment-specific configuration.

```java
package com.example.config;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Profile;

@Configuration
public class SafeAutoConfiguration {

    // SECURE: Configuration is compile-time, not runtime-loaded
    // No user input reaches bean instantiation
    // Profile-based activation replaces dynamic loading

    @Bean
    @Profile("production")
    @ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.feature.caching", havingValue = "true")
    public CacheManager productionCacheManager() {
        return new RedisCacheManager(redisConnectionFactory());
    }

    @Bean
    @Profile("development")
    public CacheManager devCacheManager() {
        // Simple in-memory cache for development
        return new ConcurrentMapCacheManager("default");
    }
}
```

**Why this works:** Spring's `@Profile` and `@ConditionalOnProperty` annotations control bean activation at startup based on environment variables and property files, not user HTTP requests. Attackers cannot influence which beans get registered.

### Tier 2: Essential Defenses (15 minutes)

Lock down Spring's auto-configuration to prevent unexpected bean registration and override attacks.

```properties
# application.properties - Production hardening

# CRITICAL: Prevent beans from being silently replaced
spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=false

# Disable circular references (can mask injection issues)
spring.main.allow-circular-references=false

# Restrict component scanning to your packages only
# Prevents classpath manipulation attacks

# Disable unnecessary auto-configurations
spring.autoconfigure.exclude=\
  org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jmx.JmxAutoConfiguration,\
  org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.admin.SpringApplicationAdminJmxAutoConfiguration,\
  org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.groovy.template.GroovyTemplateAutoConfiguration
```

**Explicit component scanning with package restrictions:**

```java
@SpringBootApplication
@ComponentScan(
    basePackages = "com.example",
    // SECURE: Only scan specific annotation types
    includeFilters = @ComponentScan.Filter(
        type = FilterType.ANNOTATION,
        classes = {Component.class, Service.class, Repository.class, Controller.class}
    ),
    // SECURE: Exclude test configs from production scanning
    excludeFilters = @ComponentScan.Filter(
        type = FilterType.REGEX,
        pattern = "com\\.example\\.test\\..*"
    )
)
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication app = new SpringApplication(Application.class);
        // Disable XML-based bean definition loading
        app.setDefaultProperties(Map.of(
            "spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding", "false"
        ));
        app.run(args);
    }
}
```

**Spring Security to block configuration manipulation endpoints:**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class AutoConfigSecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/config/**").denyAll()
                .requestMatchers("/actuator/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            );
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

### Tier 3: Production Hardening (30 minutes)

**Startup bean audit to detect unexpected registrations:**

```java
@Component
public class BeanRegistrationAuditor implements ApplicationListener<ContextRefreshedEvent> {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(BeanRegistrationAuditor.class);

    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_PACKAGES = Set.of(
        "com.example",
        "org.springframework"
    );

    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(ContextRefreshedEvent event) {
        ApplicationContext ctx = event.getApplicationContext();
        String[] beanNames = ctx.getBeanDefinitionNames();

        List<String> suspiciousBeans = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String name : beanNames) {
            try {
                Object bean = ctx.getBean(name);
                String className = bean.getClass().getName();
                boolean allowed = ALLOWED_PACKAGES.stream()
                    .anyMatch(className::startsWith);
                if (!allowed) {
                    suspiciousBeans.add(name + " -> " + className);
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Lazy beans may not resolve - log but continue
                log.debug("Could not inspect bean: {}", name);
            }
        }

        if (!suspiciousBeans.isEmpty()) {
            log.error("SECURITY ALERT: {} unexpected beans registered: {}",
                suspiciousBeans.size(), suspiciousBeans);
            // In production, consider failing startup
            if ("production".equals(System.getenv("SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE"))) {
                throw new SecurityException(
                    "Unexpected bean registrations detected in production");
            }
        }

        log.info("Bean audit complete: {} total beans, {} suspicious",
            beanNames.length, suspiciousBeans.size());
    }
}
```

**Gradle/Maven dependency verification to prevent classpath poisoning:**

```xml
<!-- pom.xml: Lock dependency versions to prevent supply chain attacks -->
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>3.4.1</version>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <id>enforce-dependency-convergence</id>
            <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
                <rules>
                    <dependencyConvergence/>
                    <banDuplicatePomDependencyVersions/>
                    <requireMavenVersion>
                        <version>[3.8.0,)</version>
                    </requireMavenVersion>
                </rules>
            </configuration>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
```

**Key operational practices:**

1. **Audit auto-configuration at startup** - Add `--debug` flag in staging to see all auto-configuration decisions, then explicitly exclude anything unnecessary with `spring.autoconfigure.exclude`.

2. **Verify bean count in CI** - Write an integration test that asserts the expected number of beans, failing if new unexpected beans appear after dependency updates.

3. **Use Spring Boot's configuration report** - Enable `/actuator/conditions` (admin-only) to review which auto-configurations activated and why.

4. **Pin all dependency versions** - Use Maven Enforcer or Gradle dependency locking to prevent transitive dependency changes that could introduce malicious auto-configurations.

5. **Container image scanning** - Scan production images with Trivy or Grype to detect known-vulnerable Spring libraries before deployment.

6. **Immutable deployments** - Use read-only filesystem mounts in Kubernetes (`readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`) to prevent runtime classpath modification.

